Let’s explore a few interesting bits about these commands.
First, two of them install binaries (mozjpeg and cwebp-bin) for mozjpeg and WebP
image encoding. As mentioned earlier, there are some security implications to using
binaries from nontrusted sources. However, both of these Node packages build the
files from source, which is good, but much of it is done out of sight from the end user.
It’s up to you to trust that the binaries they create do what you expect them to. If you
don’t want to use them, and if there is a system-wide version of the mozjpeg and
WebP tool sets already available on your machine, then you don’t need to install
either package.
Another thing that might stick out to those not familiar with Node is the different
save commands, particularly --save-dev, --save, and --global. Described most
simply, they are different ways you can manage package dependencies for your
project. The commands listed are recommended by the developers of each package,
so they should be fine defaults to start. Your Gulp workflow might require them to be
different, however, so take some time to understand the various options available to
you.
